WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.303
NARRATOR: In Luxor's
Karnak temple,

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.338
Colleen is hunting
for clues that

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:08.641
explain Ramses' rise to power.

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:11.544
Ramses was a mighty
warrior and general

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:14.814
who fought in many campaigns
and expanded Egypt's

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:18.418
borders to the east and south.

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:21.888
But the temple walls suggest
that's not the only reason

00:00:21.955 --> 00:00:24.023
he became so powerful.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.291
This is a remarkable document.

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:28.561
It's the actual text
to a peace treaty.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:32.966
NARRATOR: This 3,300-year-old
wall holds 38 lines

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:36.469
of hieroglyphic inscriptions
that lay out in detail

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:40.874
the world's first peace treaty,
an unprecedented promise

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.043
of alliance between Ramses
and his mortal enemies,

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.479
the Hittites.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:49.315
It's amazing to see the
text of an actual treaty.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:52.619
This is the document that
two great powers signed--

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:55.855
Egypt on one hand, and
the Hittites on the other.

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:59.325
NARRATOR: This proves that
Ramses didn't just fight wars,

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.294
he ended them, too.

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:04.998
Peace allowed Egypt to
prosper and for Ramses to get

00:01:05.064 --> 00:01:07.000
richer and more powerful.

00:01:07.066 --> 00:01:10.437
This tells us how neither
the Hittites nor the Egyptians

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:11.805
could attack one another.

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:14.207
And if they were attacked
by a third party,

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:17.710
they would come to
their ally's aid.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:20.046
NARRATOR: The
Egyptian-Hittite peace treaty,

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:22.282
or the Treaty of
Kadesh, meant Ramses

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:26.052
could finally end his 20-year
war with the Hittites.

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:27.554
Wars are quite costly.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.224
So by having peace, Ramses
is guaranteeing stability

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:33.827
and prosperity in his reign.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.863
NARRATOR: This allowed Ramses
to focus on gathering wealth

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.866
and building temples and
monuments that celebrated

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.367
his mighty achievements.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.736
COLLEEN DARNELL: A lot of
pharaohs, including Ramses,

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:44.737
used propaganda.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.706
But in the case of
the peace treaty,

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:50.310
these are real terms
with a real foreign king.

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:53.880
So this shows Ramses
wanted everyone to know

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.715
that he was a great diplomat.

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.784
NARRATOR: Ramses
demonstrated himself

00:01:57.851 --> 00:01:59.452
to be the ultimate king.

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:02.555
And his choice of Karnak
to proclaim as such

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:03.923
was no accident.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.426
COLLEEN DARNELL: I think Ramses
is showing the peace treaty

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:09.429
here at Karnak
precisely to contrast

00:02:09.496 --> 00:02:11.231
with his military exploits.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:14.467
He does what he needs
to do to preserve order

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:17.136
on behalf of Egypt's gods.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:20.039
NARRATOR: Ramses was a
warrior king and a diplomat

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:23.510
who ruled over an extraordinary
period of peace and prosperity.

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:26.379
[music playing]

